# SPEC: Orders & Payments

## Context
The end result is a working, production-grade e-commerce platform, built in MERN stack, written in TypeScript, featuring a clean RESTful API, shopping cart, webhook handling, and payment engineering consuming the Stripe Test API. This is an addition to the pre-existing Next.js home page and ROI calculator. Service delivers near full e-commerce experience to the user (guest only): product browsing, shopping cart management (quantity, adding/removing products), (Stripe Test) checkout, order confirmation/response via UI modal, individual order UI (consulted and authorized with token). Deliverable demonstrates clean API design, idiomatic variables, methods, and entities throughout; SOLID, SoC, DRY, KIS, and YAGNI principles; idempotent payment processing, webhook handling, retry logic, a clean layered backend (routes, controllers, services, models and tests per feature), centralized error handling (middleware), input/types and `env` validation, automated unit and integration testing (along with proper CI/CD configuration), and logging (Morgan, Winston and/or Pino). [Standing project context](./CLAUDE.md)

## 1. Outcomes (done = ?)
- On first use or without some sort of persisted session, a visitor sees a modal indicating none of their personal data is stored in system, and any persisted data is to keep their current session alive until they leave the website
- A visitor can browse products, filter by category, price range, brand, build a cart (add, modify, remove), submit checkout, complete a Stripe test payment, and receive a token that retrieves their order details in the UI
- A failed/declined card produces a modal with a user facing error message and the action required from the user (e.g.: "Try again with a different card", "The selected card has been rejected due to insuficient funds", "Invalid card", etc)
- A duplicate submission / duplicate webhook does NOT create a second order or charge
- In order to simulate real-world behavior/constraints: every purchase decrements inventory according to the quantity defined by the user (max=5 per purchase, indicated with a clear message in the UI), creates order atomically (preventing oversell); and the event of a user attempting to purchase an out-of-stock product (`isActive: false`) renders a modal stating the product is out-of-stock and the user can retry in a minute (the system automatically resets the stock to the default value of 99 and activates the product [`isActive: true`] after 25 seconds of the event)
- <!-- DEFINE: any other outcome you want to guarantee -->

## 2. Scope
### In scope
- Foundations: error middleware, ApiResponse envelope, zod env validation, zod body validation
- Orders: create, retrieve-by-token, server-derived totals, state transitions
- Payments: Stripe (Payment intents + Elements) test-mode flow, webhook handling, idempotency, retry policy
- Inventory/stock
- Authentication / user accounts
- Email/confirmation delivery
- Admin / fulfillment UI
### Out of scope
- Refunds: no real money in play, so the user wouldn't have a way to confirm
- Discounts/coupons, multi-currency

## 3. Constraints & Assumptions
- Stack: Express 5, Mongoose 8, TS/ESM, tsx runtime, Jest+ts-jest+supertest; zod (joi or yup) - new dep
- Stripe TEST (Payment Intents+Elements) mode only; no real funds, no PCI scope
- Single datastore: MongoDB; order write path uses multi-document transactions
- Currency: US dollars only, and limited to two decimals (e.g.: 49.99)
- New env vars: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, publishable key

## 4. Decisions Already Made
- Fixed non-PII identity ("John Doe"); no real email/addresses persisted for guest users
- First name and email are the sole real data persisted for registered users; mock physical address and any other data "required" for checkout/billing
- For guest users: order retrieval by an UNGUESSABLE token, not raw _id
- For registered users: order retrieval via an `Orders` UI listing their past orders by most recent with the ability to open each and see a details summary
- Response envelope: ApiResponse<T> = { success, data?, message? } on every endpoint.
- Webhook is the source of truth for payment status (not the client redirect).
- Idempotency: processed Stripe event IDs stored in a ProcessedWebhookEvent collection.
- Stripe test cards (success / decline) surfaced in the UI via their respective modals.
- Foundations built first as substrate; features conform to them from line one.
- Stripe integration shape: PaymentIntents+Elements; custom flow, payment engineering visible, stronger showcase (client secret, confirmation, more webhook surface)

